{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS002738",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All four candidates are direct matches to the target concept (alcohol dependence / alcohol use disorder): PGS002738 and PGS002739 have trait_efo=\"alcohol dependence\" with phenotyping_reported=\"Alcohol use disorder\", while PGS000201/PGS000202 are labeled \"Problematic alcohol use\" but their phenotyping_reported is also AUD (DSM-5 criteria count). Among these, PGS002738 is best supported because it has the most endpoint-faithful labeling (trait_reported=\"Alcohol use disorder\"), is developed from multi-cohort sources (MVP, UKB), and reports a reasonably strong discriminative signal via OR(top 10% vs rest)=1.96 (1.54–2.51) in a European evaluation sample (n=7,900). However, the overall evidence remains limited for a high-confidence pick because none of the candidates report PRS-comparable AUC or R2, covariates are unknown for PGS002738/PGS002739 (reducing comparability), and the remaining candidates are close competitors with similar endpoints and methods (PRS-CS) but weaker reported incremental R2 (ΔR2 0.0119 and 0.0046) and smaller/variable validation sizes."
}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS004862","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 8 candidates are direct matches to dilated cardiomyopathy by trait_efo, with generally coherent phenotype fields (either generic DCM, non-ischemic DCM, or clinical DCM). Per the provided domain guidance, phenotype alignment is primary and MTAG is not a negative; within the same study family, the MTAG-enhanced sibling is mildly preferred when endpoints/covariates are comparable.

PGS004862 (Dilated cardiomyopathy (MTAG), Nat Genet 2024-04-18) is preferred over its non-MTAG sibling PGS004861 because it has slightly stronger reported full-model discrimination (AUROC 0.71 vs 0.70; R² 0.05 vs 0.048) and a stronger per-SD effect size (OR 1.76 vs 1.56) with the same basic covariates (age, age^2, sex, PC1-10) and large European validation (n=347,585). Compared with the later 2024-12-16 family (PGS004946–PGS004951), those models have phenotype qualifiers (non-ischemic or clinical DCM) and report univariate PRS-only AUCs (~0.64–0.68), but their evaluation ancestry is listed as MAE-only for several and their reported liability-scale R² values are not clearly PRS-only and appear high relative to the provided heritability ceiling guidance, making cross-model metric comparability uncertain.

Limitation: none of the candidates provides a clearly labeled PRS-only AUROC/R² in a directly comparable way across the full set (many report only full-model AUROC/R², and others report mixed/unclear R² scales), so the choice relies on endpoint coherence plus relative effect-size support within a clean, basic-covariate, same-publication sibling comparison rather than a definitive PRS-only performance comparison."}

