  "outcome": "DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY",
  "best_model_id": "PGS004908",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All 10 candidates are direct matches to kidney cancer (or near-synonyms/clinically dominant subtype renal cell carcinoma). PGS004908 is best supported in the visible evidence because it has strong endpoint alignment (trait_reported/phenotyping_reported both “Kidney cancer”, trait_efo renal carcinoma), reports the strongest discriminative signal among candidates via effect size (OR per SD 1.5, 95% CI 1.43–1.58; domain guidance treats OR≥1.5 as strong), and shows the highest reported AUROC (0.74, EUR evaluation) among the listed models. Covariates are standard epidemiologic/demographic (age, sex, PCs, BMI, smoking, hypertension), which are not flagged as heavy clinical leakage in the provided domain knowledge. Main limitation: the AUROC appears to be a full-model metric (not explicitly PRS-only), and evaluation is EUR-only despite multi-ancestry GWAS inputs, so the PRS-only contribution and non-EUR transportability cannot be confirmed from the current metadata."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS004155",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Direct-match candidates are abundant and many are endpoint-faithful for prostate cancer, but the evidence is heterogeneous with several models targeting non-generic endpoints (e.g., aggressive/lethal/fatal disease, age-of-onset hazard scores, PSA/biopsy cohorts, or explicit 5-year/absolute-risk packaging), which limits a single clearly dominant choice for the generic target trait.\n\nPGS004155 is preferred from the visible evidence because it is a direct prostate cancer model with a large validation sample (n=171,474), reports discrimination (AUROC=0.7049) and PRS effect size (OR=2.17), and uses covariates listed as 0 (i.e., no added non-genetic covariates in the reported performance record), making the metric more PRS-comparable than candidates whose reported performance may be boosted by clinical covariates or risk-wrapper packaging.\n\nMain limitation: multiple other direct-match models also show strong performance but are not directly comparable due to differing endpoints and/or covariate structures (e.g., CanRisk-Prostate PGS003415 is a named risk model with unclear covariate packaging; PGS005238/PGS005237 report high AUROC but are explicitly 5-year incident/age-specific absolute-risk adjusted; PGS000333 reports very high full-model C-index with clinical covariates; PGS000662 reports an extreme AUROC on a lethal-10-year endpoint). This comparability conflict prevents a high-confidence, unambiguous best pick."
}

