{"outcome":"DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY","best_model_id":"PGS003381","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Direct-match candidates for the target trait (uterine cancer) are present, including umbrella uterine cancer endpoints (PGS000541, PGS000542, PGS001795, PGS003740) and the clinically dominant subtype endometrial/uterine endometrial carcinoma (PGS000075, PGS000786, PGS002300, PGS002735-PGS002737, PGS003381, PGS004244). Domain knowledge explicitly notes endometrial carcinoma as the dominant subtype of uterine carcinoma (~90% of cases), making endometrial-cancer-labeled models acceptable direct matches.

Among these, PGS003381 has strong endpoint fidelity (phenotyping_reported: uterine endometrial carcinoma), uses a genome-wide method (lassosum) with a large variant set (529,365), and reports comparatively strong full-model discrimination (AUROC 0.761) with basic covariates (age, top 20 PCs), avoiding heavy clinical leakage. Competing exact-label uterine cancer models (PGS000541/PGS000542) are sparse (18–20 variants) with lower reported AUROC (~0.57) and modest OR per SD (~1.30). The other exact-label uterine cancer framework model (PGS001795; PRS-CS-auto) has a PRS-comparable R2 (0.001948) that is below the provided heritability ceiling (h2_obs=0.0005; h2_liability=0.0116) but shows only moderate AUROC (0.66) and comes from a global biobank framework context.

Main limitation: PGS003381 does not provide PRS-only AUROC/R2 (the AUROC appears to be full-model), so the PRS contribution cannot be cleanly compared across candidates; therefore confidence is not High despite the strong endpoint/method alignment."}

